WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.819
Picture hello-devops on Kubernetes: three pods on three nodes, replaced on every deploy.

2
00:00:08.819 --> 00:00:11.183
Each pod prints its own log lines.

3
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.652
A customer says a payment failed at 14:02.

4
00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:16.277
Which pod handled it?

5
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.240
That pod may not exist any more, and its logs left with it.

6
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:23.130
Centralized logging fixes this.

7
00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:29.187
Every line every container prints is copied, as it is written, to one store you can search.

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.481
In the lab, hello-devops prints one event per line to stdout.

9
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:39.211
The access log is structured: one JSON object per line, with named fields.

10
00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:42.498
The Docker engine sees every line first.

11
00:00:42.498 --> 00:00:47.937
Its fluentd logging driver sends each one to Fluent Bit, on port 24224.

12
00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:56.429
Fluent Bit parses the JSON into fields, and sends them on to Elasticsearch, which indexes every field so search is fast.

13
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:59.045
Kibana is the web page where you search.

14
00:00:59.045 --> 00:01:02.663
The pod can be gone, and you can still read what it printed.

15
00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:08.906
Inside Elasticsearch, each log event is one JSON document.

16
00:01:08.906 --> 00:01:10.132
Here are three.

17
00:01:10.333 --> 00:01:13.505
With grep, every search reads every line again.

18
00:01:13.505 --> 00:01:18.027
Fine for one file, far too slow for a week of logs from fifty pods.

19
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:22.373
Elasticsearch does the reading once, when a document arrives.

20
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:29.071
A part called the analyzer splits each text field into words, called tokens, and lowercases them.

21
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.464
Payment FAILED becomes payment, failed, and so on.

22
00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:36.737
Then, for every token, it records which documents contain it.

23
00:01:36.737 --> 00:01:39.272
That list is called an inverted index.

24
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:44.520
The second document adds its own tokens, and its number to the rows it shares.

25
00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:47.563
It works like the index at the back of a book.

26
00:01:47.563 --> 00:01:51.808
To find declined, you look it up once, instead of reading every page.

27
00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:54.963
Search payment: one lookup, two documents.

28
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.925
Coming from grep, this is the first surprise.

29
00:01:57.925 --> 00:02:00.193
Search payments, and you get nothing.

30
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.052
Only payment was stored.

31
00:02:02.052 --> 00:02:09.075
The default analyzer does not reduce words to a common stem, so payments is a token nobody ever wrote.

32
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.827
The second surprise.

33
00:02:11.827 --> 00:02:17.631
When Redis is down, a traceback line holds redis.exceptions.ConnectionError.

34
00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:20.493
Dots between letters do not split words.

35
00:02:20.493 --> 00:02:22.753
So the whole name stays one token.

36
00:02:22.967 --> 00:02:26.027
Search ConnectionError, and you get zero hits.

37
00:02:26.027 --> 00:02:27.823
It is only part of a token.

38
00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:32.904
Search the phrase service not known, from the same error, and you find it at once.

39
00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:35.314
Not every field is split.

40
00:02:35.314 --> 00:02:41.903
A keyword field, such as level, is stored whole, capitals and all, for exact filters and counting.

41
00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:44.696
The stored value is ERROR, in capitals.

42
00:02:44.696 --> 00:02:48.376
So a term query for error, in lower case, finds nothing.

43
00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:54.841
Elasticsearch reads each log line once, when it arrives, and builds an index of whole tokens.

44
00:02:54.841 --> 00:03:00.953
So search for words that were really stored, and check the tokens before you trust an empty result.
